🔶 1️⃣ “Deferred” Is Underspecified

You allow disposition:
	•	Open
	•	Resolved
	•	Accepted Risk
	•	Deferred

But Deferred currently has no strict constraints.

Questions:
	•	Who may mark Deferred?
	•	For which domains?
	•	Does Deferred require a next trigger?
	•	Does Deferred carry into next cycle automatically?
	•	Is Deferred allowed for Strategy / Quality?

Right now Deferred could become:

“We’ll deal with it later.”

That’s governance erosion.

Recommendation

Add:
	•	Deferred requires:
	•	Explicit next trigger condition
	•	Named next engine (e.g., Roadmap Rebalance)
	•	Target checkpoint date
	•	Strategy / Quality cannot be Deferred unless lifecycle guide allows explicitly.
	•	Deferred escalations must appear in cycle_summary.md under “Carryover Governance Debt.”

Without this, Deferred becomes a silent bypass.

⸻

🔶 2️⃣ You Don’t Enforce Re-Validation on Resume After Mutation

You correctly allow resume.

But imagine:
	•	Stage 3 passed
	•	Stage 4 passed
	•	Capacity FAIL occurs
	•	Workforce escalation resolved by adjusting timebox
	•	Plan sequencing changes

You should invalidate downstream states automatically.

Right now state transitions are linear, but not invalidation-aware.

You need a rule like:

If a step modifies:
	•	stage3_execution_plan.md → invalidate stage3_5, stage4, stage4_5, stage5_5
	•	stage2_scope_extraction.md → invalidate everything downstream

Without invalidation, resume could pass stale integrity checks.

This is subtle but important.

⸻

